#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167918} :activity/id #uuid "250a993e-3838-5a89-94cf-096498708024" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049611 :topic/id #uuid "0478f594-ef93-56fe-a7bd-53928cfc55be" :topic/math-academy-id 3246} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167983 :tutorial/content "Even when a numerical method begins a step from the exact solution value, it typically does not land exactly on the true solution at the next point.\nThis discrepancy reflects how accurately the method approximates the solution over a single step.\n\nThe **local truncation error** (**LTE**) of a numerical method is the error made in a *single step* that begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_\\text{exact}(x)$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLTE isolates the intrinsic one-step accuracy of a method. It answers: \"*How well does a single step approximate the exact solution if there were no accumulated errors?*\"\n\n![](images/a8/a88ac73371bad19b7ff61128cc6f02be059702634633558dc3b868ffca15345e.png)\n\nSuppose a numerical method advances the solution using the increment formula\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\Phi(x, y, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nfor some function $\\Phi.$ Then, the LTE at step $n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Φ(x,{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x),Δx)\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)-Φ(x,{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x),Δx)\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the next slide, we will apply this formula to calculate the local truncation error for a given numerical method." :tutorial/id #uuid "ac1717ea-2f35-5789-89c2-ac4e0c02028c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14216 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3b8dff0b-9143-566d-bd38-e66c0a0807a6" :step/math-academy-id 594506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166948 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166539 :question/id #uuid "099fbdba-a319-5a75-af75-b2bbdb1b3494" :question/math-academy-id "e-21605"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9477a5ac-7e05-5fa4-9cf3-352cc17f2e1b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049441}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308413}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Local Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "61c37ff3-b7d2-5a4a-9a61-ef85e3154266" :step/math-academy-id 594507 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167920}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166949 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166538 :question/id #uuid "2d820110-30f7-5fd8-a30c-e21d45cc789f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21604"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77130223-3730-5a35-bd28-d457f079dad3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308573}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the LTE for a Numerical Method Given an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "1530ac34-bf02-59cb-ad3e-732b77d696b7" :step/math-academy-id 594510 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166950 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166511 :question/id #uuid "db842cc3-76cc-5a6c-b293-3c5f21c58002" :question/math-academy-id "e-21214"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308518}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"} :step/id #uuid "07713411-ea73-528d-a8a6-0ab7c6fa341e" :step/math-academy-id 594514 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167922}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167998 :tutorial/content "In practice, numerical methods apply many steps in sequence, using previously computed values to advance the solution. As a result, errors from earlier steps can propagate to later steps, leading to a growing discrepancy between the numerical and exact solutions over time.\n\nThe **global truncation error** (**GTE**) of a numerical method is the total error accumulated over several steps. More formally, the GTE of a numerical method after step $n$ from $x_{n-1}$ to $x_n = x_{n-1} + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\ne_n = y(x_n) - y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $y(x_n)$ is the exact value at $x_n,$ and $y_n$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nGTE is the accumulated discrepancy between the numerical and true solutions after many steps.\n\n![](images/11/116adb734a470a155bb8cc96daf912e9cacaf153c13153e1cd5e7c9687a039ad.png)\n\nNotice that, for step $1$ and initial value $y(x_0)=y_0,$ the GTE and LTE are identical:\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =y({x}_{1})-({y}_{0}+Δ{y}_{1}) \\\\  & =y({x}_{1})-(y({x}_{0})+Δ{y}_{1}) \\\\  & ={τ}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe plot below shows the numerical solution and the exact solution for a method implementation, along with the vertical gaps $e_n.$\n\n![](images/19/19a08db26ec382b193f78c5efdb3aaa3e1fc3fa459dbc50bf43b71a62943957c.png)\n\nSuppose a numerical method has increment formula\n\\[\n\\Delta y_k = \\Phi(x_{k-1}, y_{k-1}, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n\\]\nfor some function [math]\\Phi,[/math] and the next [math]y[/math]-value at step [math]k[/math] is given by\n[math]\ny_k = y_{k-1} + \\Delta y_k.\n[/math]\nThen, the GTE at step [math]n[/math] is\n\n\\begin{align*}\ne_n &= y(x_n) - y_n \\\\[5pt]\n&= y(x_n) - (y_{n-1} + \\Delta y_n) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y(x_n) - (y_{n-2} + \\Delta y_{n-1} + \\Delta y_n) \\\\[5pt]\n&\\:\\qquad\\vdots\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\quad\\vdots \\\\[5pt]\n&= y(x_n) - (y_0 + \\Delta y_1 + \\Delta y_2 + \\cdots + \\Delta y_{n-1} + \\Delta y_n) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y(x_n) - (y_0 + \\Phi(x_0, y_0, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x + \\Phi(x_1, y_1, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x + \\cdots \\\\[3pt]\n&\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad +\\: \\Phi(x_{n-2}, y_{n-2}, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x + \\Phi(x_{n-1}, y_{n-1}, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x)\n\\end{align*}\n\n* A numerical method for first-order differential equations is an iterative scheme that approximates the solution to an initial-value problem of the form\n\\[\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(x_0) = y_0\n\\]\n\n* If we want to approximate the solution over the interval from [math]x_0[/math] to [math]x_n[/math] (using [math]n[/math] steps), we divide the total length of the interval into [math]n[/math] equal-length subintervals. In other words,\n\\[\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{x_n − x_0}{n}.\n\\]\n\n* There is always a certain degree of error, and if the time interval over which the solution is appropriated is lengthened, the error will usually increase.\n\n* The rule of thumb: the smaller the step size, the more accurate the approximate solution. However, the smaller the step size, the larger the number of steps, so the longer it takes the computer to approximate the solution. It is useful to be able to predict roughly how large a step size to take in order to achieve a particular accuracy.\n\n* Let [math]y_n[/math] be the computed numerical value after [math]n[/math] steps, and [math]y(x_n)[/math] be the exact solution. Then, more precisely, for a numerical method with increment formula\n  \\[\n  \\Delta y_k = \\Phi(x_k, y_k, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n  \\]\n  where [math]\\Phi[/math] is some function and [math]\\Delta x[/math] is the step size, the GTE at step [math]n[/math] is\n  \\[\n  e_n = y(x_n) - y_n.\n  \\]" :tutorial/id #uuid "7da1eb65-5fb4-5788-b29f-815db8e2f6b2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14439 :tutorial/title "Global Truncation Error"} :step/id #uuid "521a89ce-c564-5720-8489-0c5df2bdad0e" :step/math-academy-id 594517 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167923}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166951 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166512 :question/id #uuid "b0f9cdf5-ca84-537a-9b3e-46b7c56729f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21218"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8905c41-cedb-5c7b-81f7-7639c5451174" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308633}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Global Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "4b816826-191e-59a1-978b-a492c4e33f2a" :step/math-academy-id 594518 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167999 :tutorial/content "How do local errors build to grow into global errors?\n\nAgain, suppose a numerical method advances the solution using the increment formula\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\Phi(x, y, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nfor some function $\\Phi.$\n\n- LTE $\\tau_n$ measures the error made in *one step*, where the step begins from the exact value $y(x_{n-1}).$\n\n- GTE $e_n$ measures the accumulated error after *many steps*, starting from $x_0$ and using the numerical method recursively.\n\nSo, at each step, we can think of the GTE as a combination of\n\n- the total accumulated error from the previous steps ($e_{n-1}$),\n\n- the way the method propagates these errors forward, and\n\n- a new error introduced in the current step ($\\tau_n$).\n\nIn particular, the global truncation error satisfies the following:\n\n$$\ne_n = e_{n-1} + \\left[ \\Phi(x_{n-1}, y(x_{n-1}), \\Delta x) - \\Phi(x_{n-1}, y_{n-1}, \\Delta x)\\right] \\cdot \\Delta x + \\tau_n\n$$\n\nIndeed, using the iterative notation, at step $n,$ the local truncation error is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & =y({x}_{n})-(y({x}_{n-1})+Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)\\cdot Δx).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging, we get an expression for the exact value $y(x_n){:}$\n\n$$\ny(x_n) = y(x_{n-1}) + \\Phi(x_{n-1}, y(x_{n-1}), \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x + \\tau_n\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this expression into the equation for GTE, we obtain our recursion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{n} & =y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n} \\\\  & =y({x}_{n})-({y}_{n-1}+Δ{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =[y({x}_{n-1})+Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)\\cdot Δx+{τ}_{n}]-[{y}_{n-1}+Φ({x}_{n-1},{y}_{n-1},Δx)\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & =(y({x}_{n-1})-{y}_{n-1})+(Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)-Φ({x}_{n-1},{y}_{n-1},Δx))\\cdot Δx+{τ}_{n} \\\\  & ={{{e}_{n-1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{GTE at step [math]n-1[/math]}}+{{[Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)-Φ({x}_{n-1},{y}_{n-1},Δx)]\\cdot Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{propagation of }{e}_{n-1}}+{{{τ}_{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{LTE at step [math]n[/math]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diagram below illustrates this propagation, where $\\Delta y_n$ is the increment produced by the numerical method at step $n$ starting from the previous numerical value $y_{n-1},$ while $\\Delta y$ is the increment produced by the same formula but starting from the exact value $y(x_{n-1}).$\n\n![](images/c6/c622e0a973f930cd91df87aad702c1b893f94559c599685a2ca93d4d222b4fc3.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "03dd4e68-51b9-5c32-94bd-ff468f0a71de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14442 :tutorial/title "Relationship Between Local and Global Truncation Errors"} :step/id #uuid "866fab1b-557b-5460-a1ba-eb5abb74e777" :step/math-academy-id 594521}] :activity/title "Error in Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167015}]}
